{"Q": {"id": "Q92", "question": "For an ordinary Agent turn, where must the card you play and the Agent you send come from, where do you put them, and which box of the played card do you resolve? May you instead reuse a card already in play or in your discard pile, or simply move an Agent already on the board?"}, "K": {"id": "Q92", "expected_answer": "Play one card from your hand face up in front of you and send one available Agent from your Leader to a legal board space. Gain the board-space effects and the played card’s Agent-box effects, ignoring its Reveal box during the Agent turn; a Faction board space also advances its Influence. Ordinary Agent turns do not let you play a card from your discard pile or reuse an already in-play card, and do not let you simply relocate an Agent already on the board. A separate effect such as Recall Agent can first return an Agent to your Leader for use on a later Agent turn. The ordinary space must be unoccupied, subject to the separately tested Spy exception.", "source_references": ["Main PDF p9 Player Turn—Agent Turn: card from hand face up in front; Agent from Leader to unoccupied space; board-space and Agent-box effects, ignoring Reveal box", "Main PDF p20 Recall Agent; tested exception in Q36"], "novelty": "This tests the ordinary turn’s legal sources and movement, which constrain whether a player can manufacture further Agent turns by reusing placed Agents or played/discarded cards. Q04 requests costs/card capacity/no-icon legality, Q87 requests matching access icons, Q03/Q35 request later lifetime/destinations, and Q36 requests recall reuse. None requests the ordinary origin rule. This is a distinct foundational source rule, not different numbers/names or a recombination of existing cost/access cases. The question also explicitly requests the Agent-box/ignore-Reveal-box part of the same basic procedure; Q03 asks Reveal-side card eligibility, so this makes the Agent side direct without creating a standalone reverse-box variant."}, "R": {"id": "Q92", "corrected_authoritative_source_references": [{"file": "/home/jonny/projects/boardgame-rules/extracted/dune-imperium-uprising-rules.txt", "sha256": "21342cf965b4c64456776adea86f44d4ebf60815cebae4eb6c86187bf02fb580", "lines": [403, 448]}, {"file": "/home/jonny/projects/boardgame-rules/extracted/dune-imperium-uprising-rules.txt", "sha256": "21342cf965b4c64456776adea86f44d4ebf60815cebae4eb6c86187bf02fb580", "lines": [1166, 1168]}]}, "A": {"id": "Q92", "answer": "Play one card from your hand face up and send one Agent from your Leader to a legal matching unoccupied board space; resolve its Agent box, not Reveal box. Do not reuse a card already in play or discard, or move an Agent already on board; an Agent turn places an Agent from Leader.", "support": "supported", "evidence": "Agent Turn."}, "B": {"id": "Q92", "answer": "Play one card from your hand face up in front of you and send one of your Agents from your Leader to an unoccupied matching space. Resolve that card's Agent box and ignore its Reveal box. You cannot reuse a card already in play or discard pile for this turn, and you cannot simply move an Agent already on the board; an Agent turn sends one from the Leader, except a recalled other Agent can later be used again as an Agent on another turn.", "support": "supported", "evidence": "Agent Turn, lines 95, 100; Recall Agent line 185."}}
{"Q": {"id": "Q93", "question": "Shaddam Corrino IV plays Signet Ring to send an Agent to a Combat space. May he choose to deploy units before Emperor of the Known Universe’s deployment restriction starts by resolving the board-space effects first, or does the restriction already apply when he places the Agent?"}, "K": {"id": "Q93", "expected_answer": "The restriction takes effect immediately when Signet Ring is played to place the Agent, so it already applies to deployment on that turn. General freedom to order board-space and Agent-card effects cannot postpone it. The restriction lasts only for the turn in which it was triggered.", "source_references": ["Main PDF p17 Clarifications—Shaddam Corrino IV: restriction takes effect immediately on Signet Ring Agent placement", "Main PDF p9 ordinary freedom to order board/card/Influence effects", "FAQ PDF p3 Shaddam Corrino IV: only triggered turn, not remaining round"], "novelty": "Consequential named exception to general Agent-effect ordering: onset has priority over deployment. Q43 requests duration but not onset or ordering; Q72 asks generic Signet activation versus Reveal; Q24 asks normal ordering and pre-entry prerequisites. These do not directly request whether the restriction can be postponed by ordering. This adds an explicitly source-defined timing/priority exception, not another duration or generic Signet variant."}, "R": {"id": "Q93", "corrected_authoritative_source_references": [{"file": "/home/jonny/projects/boardgame-rules/extracted/dune-imperium-uprising-rules.txt", "sha256": "21342cf965b4c64456776adea86f44d4ebf60815cebae4eb6c86187bf02fb580", "lines": [948, 951]}, {"file": "/home/jonny/projects/boardgame-rules/docs/quality/summary-qa/dune-imperium-uprising/2026-11-04-batch-231507/dune-imperium-official-faq-2025-01-13.txt", "sha256": "783b74f6aea9b4f0512e68a0581ca0438cb42dd565e2d88e0778b1322ecb5f23", "lines": [297, 300]}]}, "A": {"id": "Q93", "answer": "The restriction applies immediately when Signet Ring is played to send the Agent; resolving board effects first cannot postpone it.", "support": "supported", "evidence": "Leader clarifications—Shaddam: restriction takes effect immediately; ordering freedom cannot postpone it."}, "B": {"id": "Q93", "answer": "No. Emperor of the Known Universe's deployment restriction applies immediately when Shaddam plays Signet Ring to send an Agent; resolving the board-space effects first cannot delay it.", "support": "supported", "evidence": "Leader clarifications line 188; FAQ Shaddam line 284."}}
{"Q": {"id": "Q94", "question": "In a four-player Conflict, all four players have different positive strengths and no sandworms. Which ranks receive each printed reward, which rank receives no reward, and who takes the Conflict card?"}, "K": {"id": "Q94", "expected_answer": "The strongest player wins and receives the first reward, second strongest receives the second reward, third strongest receives the third reward, and fourth strongest receives no reward. Only the winner takes the Conflict card face up into their supply and checks for a battle-icon match.", "source_references": [{"file": "/home/jonny/projects/boardgame-rules/docs/quality/summary-qa/dune-imperium-uprising/2026-11-04-batch-231507/dune-imperium-uprising-rules.txt", "lines": [823, 839], "pdf_page": 14}, {"file": "/home/jonny/projects/boardgame-rules/extracted/dune-imperium-uprising-rules.txt", "lines": [751, 765], "pdf_page": 14}], "novelty": "Source-defined player-count branch: in four players the untied third-place finisher receives the third reward; in three or fewer this reward is used only for a tie for second. Q30 requests only the untied three-player case and two humans plus Rival. Q09 requests tied first in 4P; Q29 requests tied third and three-way tied first. Neither asks the untied 4P allocation. This is a different rulebook reward branch, not arbitrary changed numbers within an existing branch. Winner-card background is included for a self-contained answer but is not claimed as new coverage."}, "R": {"id": "Q94", "corrected_authoritative_source_references": [{"file": "/home/jonny/projects/boardgame-rules/extracted/dune-imperium-uprising-rules.txt", "sha256": "21342cf965b4c64456776adea86f44d4ebf60815cebae4eb6c86187bf02fb580", "lines": [750, 765]}]}, "A": {"id": "Q94", "answer": "First, second, and third receive the corresponding printed rewards; fourth gets none. Highest-strength player wins and takes the Conflict card.", "support": "supported", "evidence": "Resolve Combat: four-player normal first, second, third rewards; winner takes card."}, "B": {"id": "Q94", "answer": "With four players at distinct positive strengths and no worms, first place receives the printed first reward, second receives second, and third receives third; fourth place receives no reward. The first-place winner takes the Conflict card, then checks for a battle-icon match.", "support": "supported", "evidence": "Combat, lines 105-108: four-player ranks, zero strength no reward, winner takes card then checks match."}}
{"Q": {"id": "Q95", "question": "During standard setup, must human Leaders be distributed at random, or may the players choose them?"}, "K": {"id": "Q95", "expected_answer": "Each player takes a Leader and places it in front of them; Leaders may be chosen or selected at random. Shaddam remains subject to the CHOAM restriction already tested in Q21.", "source_references": [{"file": "/home/jonny/projects/boardgame-rules/docs/quality/summary-qa/dune-imperium-uprising/2026-11-04-batch-231507/dune-imperium-uprising-rules.txt", "lines": [205, 208], "pdf_page": 4}, {"file": "/home/jonny/projects/boardgame-rules/extracted/dune-imperium-uprising-rules.txt", "lines": [193, 195], "pdf_page": 4}], "novelty": "An actual setup permission not requested anywhere in the 93-question bank. Q21 explicitly requests selection/distribution of Objective cards and only the Shaddam restriction for Leaders. Q44 requests particular Leader initial states; Q84 requests deck and component preparation; neither asks how Leaders are selected. Choosing versus compulsory random assignment is a different source rule from random Objective distribution, not changed names or numbers in an existing branch."}, "R": {"id": "Q95", "corrected_authoritative_source_references": [{"file": "/home/jonny/projects/boardgame-rules/extracted/dune-imperium-uprising-rules.txt", "sha256": "21342cf965b4c64456776adea86f44d4ebf60815cebae4eb6c86187bf02fb580", "lines": [193, 195]}]}, "A": {"id": "Q95", "answer": "Players may choose a Leader or select one at random.", "support": "supported", "evidence": "Setup step 3: choose a Leader (choose, or select at random)."}, "B": {"id": "Q95", "answer": "Players may choose a Leader or select one at random; random distribution is not required.", "support": "supported", "evidence": "Setup, line 54: each player takes a Leader (choose, or select at random)."}}
{"Q": {"id": "Q96", "question": "When an ordinary draw-a-card effect resolves, which deck supplies the card? May you choose a card directly from your discard pile instead?"}, "K": {"id": "Q96", "expected_answer": "Draw from your own deck. You do not choose a card directly from your discard pile; when the deck is empty and a draw is needed, reshuffle the discard pile into a new deck and continue drawing.", "source_references": [{"file": "/home/jonny/projects/boardgame-rules/docs/quality/summary-qa/dune-imperium-uprising/2026-11-04-batch-231507/dune-imperium-uprising-rules.txt", "lines": [1239, 1242], "pdf_page": 20}, {"file": "/home/jonny/projects/boardgame-rules/docs/quality/summary-qa/dune-imperium-uprising/2026-11-04-batch-231507/dune-imperium-uprising-rules.txt", "lines": [338, 339], "pdf_page": 6}], "novelty": "The ordinary draw icon has an explicit card origin. Q23/Q80 request when round-start draws happen; Q39 requests during/after-Reveal timing; Q34 requests when reshuffling occurs. None requests which deck an ordinary draw effect uses. Q78 requests Intrigue deck versus opponent origins, a different draw mechanism. Existing board-space effects questions request that a card is drawn, but not the ordinary draw source or permission to select from discard. Only this origin is claimed as new; the reshuffle explanation repeats Q34 to make the answer usable."}, "R": {"id": "Q96", "corrected_authoritative_source_references": [{"file": "/home/jonny/projects/boardgame-rules/extracted/dune-imperium-uprising-rules.txt", "sha256": "21342cf965b4c64456776adea86f44d4ebf60815cebae4eb6c86187bf02fb580", "lines": [301, 302]}, {"file": "/home/jonny/projects/boardgame-rules/extracted/dune-imperium-uprising-rules.txt", "sha256": "21342cf965b4c64456776adea86f44d4ebf60815cebae4eb6c86187bf02fb580", "lines": [1132, 1133]}]}, "A": {"id": "Q96", "answer": "Draw ordinary cards from your own deck, reshuffling your discard pile into a new deck if needed. You cannot choose directly from discard.", "support": "supported", "evidence": "Drawing cards."}, "B": {"id": "Q96", "answer": "An ordinary draw-a-card effect draws from your own deck. You may not choose a card directly from your discard pile; if your deck is empty, shuffle the discard pile into a new deck and continue drawing.", "support": "supported", "evidence": "Drawing cards, line 167."}}

